#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172040 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171846 :question/id #uuid "3602a14d-6bd7-5586-a6e8-51d069dc8641" :question/math-academy-id "e-8507" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = x+y?$ \n\n![](images/3b/3bd19865d2a1bb6bbb40c57112f269b51a3740d01da2c80b81db4eb84c6766f1.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/54/543bb98ff948d1186e05d84db7992f9d5033ba4eaf47a9787b6b5664b88510a2.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](images/43/433f0cf3835465e7a0cca195f8b0f0b280984200d84f49533219f08967adc497.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2334101-5266-5853-aedf-04c85746b5cc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048179 :topic/math-academy-id 3351 :topic/title "Slope Fields for Autonomous Differential Equations"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e7544c8-1881-562c-a26c-67a5f3ad2076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b3/b3e3fd451b0ebb2b4eb614dd54f5193be179f8bdcf7d84191004ddb80e39b3f8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358016} {:answer/id #uuid "4b404c6e-5559-5f02-84c2-72074650e04f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a3/a324b98fc4ec7a38ce6276a357a649751b316c0e61ddb193ce5f34caed608e98.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358397} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc84bf1-4325-5c89-9a0a-6f20097f7793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/valu